#---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Isotropic exchange and Dzyaloshinskii-Moriya (DM) values -> Bo et al: https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1367-2630/acb3ee/meta.
# The aforementioned values are rescaled to have a solid balance between exchange/DM values and the anisotropy, since anisotropy is taken 
# from Wang et al: https://pubs.aip.org/aip/apl/article/117/8/083102/39740. In this regard, we go to the supplementary material, Table S1, of 
# Esteras et al: https://pubs.acs.org/doi/full/10.1021/acs.nanolett.2c02863. In that table, Wang's value for J2 is J2=6.23 meV, and it is the 
# isotropic intralayer dominant exchange link, as in Bo's, where J2=3.51 meV. Bo et al do not provide the functional form of the configurational 
# energy, so we do not know if the listed exchange parameters in Table 2 are given taking into account double counting, if the associated 
# magnetization vectors have length or not... same for the DM values reported in the aforementioned paper. So, basically, we multiply all the 
# exchange and DM values by (2*6.23/3.51). Regarding the characterization of DM vectors mediating the intralayer J1 and J3 links, we used the 
# accurate magnetic and non-magnetic atoms positions given in Wilson's paper. However, it has to be taken into account that doing so the DM vectors
# are not completely along b-th (for J1) and a-th (for J3) axes. So we neglect the non-zero components that are not along the directions stated
# by Bo's, and we transform the dominant DM vector to be completely consistent with the aforementioned paper. Wilson's positions, in any case, are
# useful for the construction of the interaction list shown below, since the relative positions of the magnetic and non-magnetic atoms dictates
# the sign-dependent DM vectors mediating the different links for J1 and J3 for each i-th Cr atom
#---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Interactions 
